      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best outdoor concrete stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most patios, a water-based semi-transparent stain such as Behr's is the best balance of predictable colour and easy application. For the mottled natural-stone look, a reactive acid stain like Kemiko Stone Tone produces depth no water-based product matches. For a slab that is blotchy, patched or oil-stained, a solid colour acrylic is the only thing that will look uniform.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will concrete stain work on old concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually yes, provided the slab is clean, unsealed and structurally sound. Old concrete often takes water-based stains particularly well because the surface is open. Acid stains are less predictable on very old slabs, where much of the free lime the reaction depends on has already carbonated away — which is why a test patch matters.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you have to seal concrete after staining?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Stain provides colour, not protection. Without a sealer the colour weathers, dirt embeds in the open surface, and acid-stained slabs in particular can look dusty within a season. Two thin coats of a suitable exterior sealer after the stain has fully dried is the standard finish.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Acid stain or water-based stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Acid stain reacts with the concrete, producing permanent, variegated, stone-like colour that cannot peel — but the result depends on the slab and cannot be predicted precisely. Water-based stain gives the colour you chose from a chart, works on slabs acid stain struggles with, and is far easier to handle. Choose acid for character, water-based for certainty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does outdoor concrete stain last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The colour from an acid stain is permanent because it is chemically part of the slab; what wears is the sealer over it, typically every two to four years. Water-based stains hold colour well under a maintained sealer. Solid colour acrylics are films and wear in traffic paths, usually needing a recoat every three to five years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you stain concrete that has been sealed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not until the sealer is removed. Stains have to reach the concrete, and any existing sealer blocks them completely. Test with water: if it beads rather than soaking in within thirty seconds, a sealer is present and must be stripped or ground off before staining.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does concrete stain hide cracks and stains?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Transparent and semi-transparent stains hide almost nothing — they colour the surface while every existing mark, patch and repair shows through, and on a variegated finish that can even look intentional. Solid colour stains cover completely. Neither fills or repairs a crack, which has to be dealt with before staining.",
