          Concrete stain colours the slab you already have rather than covering it, which is why a stained patio looks like stone and a painted one looks like paint. The catch is that the slab is a participant: its age, its finish and whatever is already on it decide how much colour you get.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="three-types" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. The Three Types of Concrete Stain
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reactive Acid Stains</h3>
        <p>
          Metallic salts in a mild acid solution that react chemically with free lime in the concrete. The colour becomes part of the slab, so it cannot peel, flake or wear off — it can only be ground away. The look is variegated and translucent, with the mottling that makes stained concrete resemble natural stone, and no two slabs come out the same. The palette is limited to earth tones: browns, tans, rusts, and blue-greens.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade-off is predictability. The reaction depends on the concrete's chemistry, so an old slab with little free lime left, or one with a hard troweled surface, may take colour weakly or unevenly. Acid handling also demands gloves, eye protection and care, and the surface has to be neutralised and rinsed thoroughly before anything else goes on it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based Penetrating Stains</h3>
        <p>
          Fine pigments in a water carrier that soak into the pore structure and deposit colour without a chemical reaction. The colour you pick is close to the colour you get, the range extends far beyond earth tones, and they work on slabs where acid staining would struggle — including newer concrete and previously ground surfaces.
        </p>
        <p>
          They produce less depth than a reactive stain unless deliberately layered, which is why the better exterior products are designed to be blended. There is no acid to handle, no neutralising step, and cleanup is water.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solid Colour Stains</h3>
        <p>
          Effectively a thin, tough paint: opaque acrylic that covers the concrete completely and delivers a uniform colour. The reason to choose one is a slab that no transparent product can rescue — patched, oil-ghosted, or made of two pours that never matched.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because it is a film on the surface, it wears in traffic paths and eventually needs recoating, and the concrete no longer looks like concrete. That is a fair trade on a tired driveway and the wrong choice on a slab with any character worth keeping. Our <a href="/best-driveway-paint-reviews/">driveway paint guide</a> covers this end of the category in more depth.
        </p>

        <h2 id="will-it-work" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Will It Work on Your Slab?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Four checks, in the order that matters. Any one of them can rule out staining entirely, and all four take an afternoon.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Is it sealed?</strong> Drop water on several areas. If it beads or sits rather than soaking in within about thirty seconds, there is a sealer present and no stain will penetrate. It has to be stripped or ground off first, which is a real job — our guide to <a href="/sealing-old-concrete/">sealing old concrete</a> covers identifying and removing what is already there.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>How old is it?</strong> New concrete needs a full cure, typically at least 28 days, before staining. Very old concrete usually takes water-based stain well but may respond weakly to acid stain, because the free lime the reaction needs has largely carbonated over the decades.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>How was it finished?</strong> A hard power-troweled surface is dense and closed, and stains struggle to penetrate it. A broom finish or a light float finish is open and takes colour readily. If water sits on an unsealed slab, the surface may simply be too tight, and a light grind or an etch is needed to open it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>What is on it?</strong> Oil, grease, efflorescence, paint spots, adhesive residue and even leaf tannin will all resist the stain and show up as pale patches. Every one of them has to come off first — and oil in particular goes deeper than it looks, as our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> explains.
        </p>

        <!-- Product Reviews -->
