          ))}
        </div>

        <p>
          One more consideration before you commit: what happens if you dislike the result. A water-based or solid colour stain can be gone over with a darker colour, or covered entirely with a solid, so a disappointing outcome is recoverable. An acid stain is not — the colour is inside the concrete, and the only routes out are covering it with an opaque product or grinding the surface away. That asymmetry is the strongest practical argument for testing thoroughly before acid staining a large area, and for choosing a water-based product if you are at all unsure.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Preparation Decides the Result
        </h2>

        <p>
          More than with almost any other coating, what you do before the stain comes out determines what you end up looking at for the next decade. Stain is transparent; the slab underneath is the picture.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Strip any sealer.</strong> Chemically or mechanically, completely and evenly. Patchy sealer removal produces patchy colour, and the patches follow the shape of wherever the stripper worked best.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Degrease individually, then clean the whole slab.</strong> Treat oil spots one at a time with a degreaser or poultice before washing the whole surface, or you simply spread thin oil across a wider area.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Pressure wash with a fan tip, not a turbo nozzle.</strong> A rotating nozzle held close cuts into the surface paste and leaves wand marks that the stain will highlight rather than hide. Keep the tip moving and stand back.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Repair cracks before, not after.</strong> Patches will not take stain the same way the slab does, so plan for them to show. On a variegated acid-stained finish that can read as character; on a uniform water-based colour it reads as a repair. Either way, repair first and let the material cure fully.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry completely.</strong> At least 24 hours after washing, longer in cool or humid weather. Damp concrete dilutes the stain and produces weak, uneven colour.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Applying and Sealing
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Test first, always.</strong> A square yard in a corner, taken all the way through to the sealer, tells you what the whole patio will look like. Stain colour changes dramatically once sealed — usually deepening substantially — so a test that stops before sealing tells you very little.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply with a sprayer, work with a brush.</strong> Most stains go on best with a plastic pump sprayer, worked in with a broom or brush where coverage needs evening out. Avoid metal sprayers with acid stains, for obvious reasons.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep edges wet and work in irregular sections.</strong> Straight stop lines show. Working to a natural break — a control joint, an edge — or feathering into an irregular boundary keeps the finish continuous.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Neutralise acid stains.</strong> A baking soda or ammonia solution, then a thorough rinse until the water runs clear, and a full dry. Residual acid under a sealer will cause it to fail.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Seal with two thin coats.</strong> Sealer is what protects the colour from UV, traffic and dirt, and it is also what determines the final appearance — matte, satin or wet look. On anything walked on when wet, choose matte or add a slip-resistant additive, because a glossy sealed slab is genuinely slippery. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers the options.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much Product and How Long It Takes</h3>
        <p>
          Coverage varies with porosity more than with the label. As a working figure, a gallon of water-based stain covers roughly 200 to 400 square feet on a typical patio slab, and acid stain concentrates go considerably further once diluted. Sealer is the bigger consumable: two coats over a 400-square-foot patio takes two to three gallons of most exterior sealers, and it is worth buying it all in one batch.
        </p>
        <p>
          On time, a realistic weekend looks like this. Saturday morning: strip, degrease and pressure wash. Saturday afternoon: repairs, and let everything dry. Sunday morning: stain, and for acid stain, neutralise and rinse. Sunday afternoon and into Monday: dry thoroughly, then two coats of sealer with the recoat window between them. Anyone compressing that into a single day is skipping a drying step, and drying steps are the ones that show up later as blotches.
        </p>

        <h2 id="colour" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Choosing a Colour
        </h2>

        <p>
          Two rules save most of the regret. First, the colour on the chart is the colour in a lab; the colour on your slab is that filtered through whatever grey the concrete already is, plus the darkening that arrives with the sealer. Pale, warm tones shift the least; blues and greens shift the most.
        </p>

        <p>
          Second, look at the fixed things around the slab rather than at the chart. The house's brick or render, the fence, the roof and any adjacent paving are all staying put. Earth tones — tan, mid brown, weathered grey — sit comfortably against almost all of them and are the reason those colours dominate the category.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the goal is to imitate flagstone or slate rather than simply to add colour, the technique matters as much as the product: multiple tones, applied unevenly, with cut joint lines. Our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-stain-concrete-patio-to-look-like-stone/">staining concrete to look like stone</a> covers that process end to end.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keeping It Looking Good</h3>
        <p>
          A stained slab is maintained through its sealer rather than through the stain. Sweep grit off regularly — it is abrasive and wears the sealer in traffic paths faster than weather does — and rinse the surface a few times a season, particularly under trees where leaf tannins stain and algae takes hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          Watch the water test on the sealer the same way you would on any sealed concrete: when water stops beading and starts darkening the surface, the sealer has worn thin and a fresh coat is due. Catching that early means a clean recoat over a sound surface; leaving it means dirt embeds in the exposed stain and the recoat locks it in. In cold climates, keep de-icing salt off a stained patio entirely — it attacks the sealer first and the surface paste after that, and the damage shows as pale, dusty patches exactly where the colour was strongest.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Testing Through to the Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer changes stained concrete dramatically, usually deepening it by a shade or more. A test patch that stops at the stain tells you almost nothing about the finished appearance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Over a Sealed Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain must reach the concrete. Over an existing sealer it sits on top, dries unevenly and wipes off in places. Thirty seconds with a cup of water settles the question before you buy anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Transparent Stain to Hide Damage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patches, oil ghosts and mismatched pours show straight through. If the slab is too marked for that, the honest choice is a solid colour rather than hoping the stain will disguise it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Neutralisation After Acid Staining</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Residual acid left under a sealer causes it to fail, sometimes months later. Neutralise, rinse until the runoff is clear, and let the slab dry completely before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Glossy Sealer Where People Walk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet-look sealers make colour sing and make a wet patio slippery. Around pools, on steps, or anywhere with older visitors, use a matte finish or have a slip-resistant additive mixed in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best outdoor concrete stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For most patios, a water-based semi-transparent like Behr's balances predictable colour with easy application. For mottled, natural-stone depth, a reactive acid stain such as Kemiko Stone Tone is unmatched. For a blotchy, patched or oil-marked slab, only a solid colour acrylic will look uniform.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will stain work on old concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually, if the slab is clean, unsealed and sound. Old concrete often takes water-based stain very well because the surface is open. Acid stain is less predictable on very old slabs, where much of the free lime it reacts with has carbonated away — hence the test patch.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to seal afterwards?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — stain gives colour, not protection. Unsealed, the colour weathers and dirt embeds in the open surface, and acid-stained slabs can look dusty within a season. Two thin coats of a suitable exterior sealer once the stain is fully dry is the standard finish.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Acid or water-based?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Acid reacts with the slab, giving permanent variegated colour that cannot peel but cannot be predicted precisely. Water-based gives the colour you chose, works where acid struggles, and is far easier to handle. Acid for character, water-based for certainty.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Acid stain colour is permanent because it is part of the slab; what wears is the sealer, typically every two to four years. Water-based stains hold well under a maintained sealer. Solid colour films wear in traffic paths and usually want recoating every three to five years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain sealed concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Not until the sealer comes off — stain has to reach the concrete and any sealer blocks it completely. Test with water: beading rather than absorbing within thirty seconds means a sealer is present and needs stripping or grinding first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does stain hide cracks and marks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Transparent and semi-transparent stains hide almost nothing; every patch and mark shows through, though on a variegated finish that can look intentional. Solid colour covers completely. Neither repairs a crack — that has to be dealt with before staining.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is stained concrete slippery?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The stain does not change traction; the sealer over it can. A glossy film on a smooth slab is genuinely slippery when wet, which matters on steps and around pools. Choose a matte or penetrating sealer, or have a slip-resistant additive mixed in.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

