        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most outdoor slabs, a <strong class="text-white">water-based semi-transparent stain</strong> is the right call — predictable, easy and available with the sealer alongside it. Choose a <strong class="text-white">reactive acid stain</strong> when you want variegated, permanent, stone-like colour and are prepared for the slab to have opinions. Choose a <strong class="text-white">solid colour</strong> only when the concrete is too marked for anything transparent to save.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then put the effort where it decides the outcome: strip any sealer, degrease properly, test a square yard all the way through to the sealer, and finish with two thin coats. Stain is transparent, so the slab underneath is the picture — and the preparation is what you are actually painting with.
          </p>
